    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-buddhist">Hindu-Buddhist</a> kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-java">West Java</a>, ruled in its last century from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakuan-pajajaran">Pakuan Pajajaran</a> on the site of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogor">Bogor</a>.</li><li>It exported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunda-kelapa">Sunda Kalapa</a>, the port that became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jakarta">Jakarta</a>, and resisted both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a> and the Muslim coastal states.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banten">Banten</a>’s capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakuan-pajajaran">Pakuan</a> in 1579 ended the kingdom, but Sundanese language, literature and identity endure in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-java">West Java</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The stone pillar set up for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunda-strait">Sunda</a>’s 1522 treaty with Portugal was rediscovered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jakarta">Jakarta</a> in 1918 and is now kept in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-museum-of-indonesia">National Museum of Indonesia</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

