    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/purnavarman"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/purnavarman.jpg" alt="Purnavarman" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/purnavarman">Purnavarman</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ilustrasi_Purnawarman.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Uncredited, published by Wacana Nusantara, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Sunda Kingdom occupied the western third of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, the land of the Sundanese people, which in the fifth century had been the seat of Tarumanagara, known from the inscriptions of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/purnavarman">Purnavarman</a>. Later Sundanese tradition dates the kingdom’s foundation to 669, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarusbawa">Tarusbawa</a> is said to have taken the western part of the former Tarumanagara lands while the eastern part, beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/citarum-river">Citarum River</a>, became the Galuh Kingdom. This account comes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> written centuries afterwards, some of disputed authenticity, and the early history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunda-strait">Sunda</a> remains largely uncertain. The earliest securely dated record naming a king of Sunda is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kebon-kopi-ii-inscription">Kebon Kopi II inscription</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogor">Bogor</a>, generally read as dated to 932, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanghyang-tapak-inscription">Sanghyang Tapak inscriptions</a> of 1030 near Sukabumi record a ruler called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-jayabupati">Sri Jayabupati</a> who declared a stretch of river sacred and forbade fishing there. The kingdom’s centre shifted between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakuan-pajajaran">Pakuan</a>, near modern Bogor, and Kawali in the Galuh lands, where inscriptions commemorate the long-reigning king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niskala-wastu-kancana">Niskala Wastu Kancana</a> in the fifteenth century. The crowns of Sunda and Galuh were often held by one ruler, and around 1482 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-baduga-maharaja">Sri Baduga Maharaja</a> united them permanently and ruled from Pakuan Pajajaran. By the early sixteenth century the Portuguese apothecary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tome-pires">Tomé Pires</a> described Sunda as a kingdom extending from the Sunda Strait east to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimanuk-river">Cimanuk River</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tangerang"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tangerang.jpg" alt="Tangerang" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tangerang">Tangerang</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Azhom_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">JahlilMA, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunda-strait">Sunda</a> was an agrarian kingdom, but its farming differed from that of central and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>. Much of the Sundanese highland was cultivated by shifting dry-field <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> farming, known as <em>huma</em>, rather than by the irrigated rice terraces that sustained dense populations further east, and the population was correspondingly thinner. The kingdom’s great export was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a>. The Chinese official <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhao-rugu">Zhao Rugu</a> noted around 1225 that Sunda produced pepper of high quality, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tome-pires">Tomé Pires</a>, writing about 1512–1515, listed its ports, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banten">Banten</a>, Pontang, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangerang">Tangerang</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunda-kelapa">Kalapa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimanuk-river">Cimanuk</a>, which shipped pepper, rice and other goods to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> and beyond. Sunda Kalapa, at the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ciliwung-river">Ciliwung River</a>, was the most important harbour and served the capital upriver. Religion combined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> and Buddhist teaching with older Sundanese reverence for ancestors and sacred places, the <em>kabuyutan</em>, which were protected sites of worship and learning. Old Sundanese literature preserves this world: the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanghyang-siksa-kandang-karesian">Sanghyang Siksa Kandang Karesian</a></em>, dated 1518, sets out moral and practical knowledge for different ranks of society, and the poem of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bujangga-manik">Bujangga Manik</a>, a Sundanese prince turned hermit who travelled through Java and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a>, describes the landscape and religious sites of the late fifteenth century. Its palm-leaf <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> has been held in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodleian-library">Bodleian Library</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> since 1627.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-bubat"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-bubat.jpg" alt="Battle of Bubat" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-bubat">Battle of Bubat</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bubat_war_statue.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Historian128, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Little is known of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunda-strait">Sunda</a>’s armies, but they were land forces drawn from the nobility and their followers, and the kingdom does not appear to have maintained a significant war fleet. Its most famous military episode is a tragedy. In 1357, according to the Javanese <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pararaton">Pararaton</a></em> and the later poem <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kidung-sunda">Kidung Sunda</a></em>, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linggabuana">Linggabuana</a> travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a> with his daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dyah-pitaloka">Dyah Pitaloka</a>, who was to marry King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hayam-wuruk">Hayam Wuruk</a>. When the Majapahit chief minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gajah-mada">Gajah Mada</a> demanded that the princess be presented as a tribute of submission rather than as a bride, the Sundanese party refused and was destroyed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-bubat">Battle of Bubat</a>; the princess is said to have taken her own life. Sunda nonetheless appears never to have been brought under Majapahit rule. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-baduga-maharaja">Sri Baduga Maharaja</a> strengthened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakuan-pajajaran">Pakuan Pajajaran</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batutulis-inscription">Batutulis inscription</a> records that he surrounded it with a moat. The decisive threat of the sixteenth century came from Muslim states on the north coast. In 1522 Sunda made a treaty with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-malacca">Portuguese of Malacca</a>, allowing them to build a fort at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunda-kelapa">Sunda Kalapa</a> in return for help, but before the Portuguese returned, forces from Demak and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirebon">Cirebon</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatahillah">Fatahillah</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banten">Banten</a> and, in 1527, Sunda Kalapa itself, defeating a Portuguese landing party. The kingdom fought on inland for half a century against the new Banten Sultanate.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/batutulis-inscription"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/batutulis-inscription.jpg" alt="Batutulis inscription" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/batutulis-inscription">Batutulis inscription</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Batutulis_Inscription_inside.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RXerself, Creutzen, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunda-strait">Sunda</a> was ruled by a king styled <em>prabu</em> or <em>maharaja</em>, whose authority rested on lineage, on religious sanction and on the loyalty of regional lords. The dual crowns of Sunda and Galuh reflected a long-standing division between west and east, and later Sundanese chronicles describe frequent shifts of the royal seat and succession by sons, brothers and sons-in-law. Inscriptions show kings regulating sacred lands: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> plates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-baduga-maharaja">Sri Baduga Maharaja</a> found at Kebantenan exempt religious communities from taxes and labour dues, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanghyang-tapak-inscription">Sanghyang Tapak inscriptions</a> protect a sacred stretch of river under threat of curses. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batutulis-inscription">Batutulis inscription</a> of 1533, set up by Sri Baduga’s son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/surawisesa">Surawisesa</a>, praises his father for building the moat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakuan-pajajaran">Pakuan</a>, raising an artificial hill, creating a sacred forest and constructing a lake, works that combined defence, ritual and public display. Popular tradition identifies Sri Baduga with the legendary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prabu-siliwangi">Prabu Siliwangi</a>, the model king of Sundanese storytelling, though the identification cannot be proven. Ports were governed by royal officials who collected dues from traders, and outlying areas such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirebon">Cirebon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumedang">Sumedang</a> Larang were held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> lords. Cirebon, ruled from the late fifteenth century by the Muslim teacher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunan-gunung-jati">Sunan Gunung Jati</a>, broke away by refusing tribute, an early sign of how coastal conversion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> could detach the kingdom’s richest territories from its control.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bujangga-manik"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bujangga-manik.jpg" alt="Bujangga Manik" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bujangga-manik">Bujangga Manik</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bujangga_Manik.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bujangga Manik, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Sunda Kingdom shaped the identity of the Sundanese, today one of the largest ethnic groups in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, who form the majority of the population of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-java">West Java</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banten">Banten</a>. Old Sundanese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> and inscriptions, from the Kawali stones to the poem of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bujangga-manik">Bujangga Manik</a>, are the foundation of Sundanese literary history, and the figure of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prabu-siliwangi">Prabu Siliwangi</a> remains central to Sundanese oral tradition, the <em>pantun</em> recited by bards. The Indonesian army’s West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> command bears the name Siliwangi. The kingdom’s capital lay on the site of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogor">Bogor</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batutulis-inscription">Batutulis</a> still stands in the city. Its principal port, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunda-kelapa">Sunda Kalapa</a>, became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jakarta">Jayakarta</a> after the Muslim conquest and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavia">Batavia</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, and Jakarta dates its foundation to 22 June 1527, the traditional date of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatahillah">Fatahillah</a>’s victory. The stone pillar, or <em>padrão</em>, erected for the 1522 treaty with Portugal was rediscovered in Jakarta in 1918 and is kept in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-museum-of-indonesia">National Museum of Indonesia</a>, one of the earliest records of European diplomacy in the archipelago. The memory of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-bubat">Battle of Bubat</a> long coloured relations between Sundanese and Javanese. Some communities, notably the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baduy">Baduy</a> of the Banten highlands, maintain an ancestral religion often linked to pre-Islamic Sundanese belief, and scholars use their customs to help interpret the kingdom’s religious practice.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sultan-hasanuddin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sultan-hasanuddin.jpg" alt="Sultan Hasanuddin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sultan-hasanuddin">Sultan Hasanuddin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MonumentSultanHasanuddin.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Torbenbrinker, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunda-strait">Sunda</a>’s decline followed the spread of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> along the north coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> in the fifteenth and sixteenth centuries. Muslim merchants and teachers established communities in the ports, and once <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirebon">Cirebon</a> had broken away and Demak had become the leading power of central Java, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-buddhist">Hindu-Buddhist</a> kingdom lost control of the harbours that provided its trade revenue. The Portuguese alliance of 1522 came too late to help, and the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banten">Banten</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunda-kelapa">Sunda Kalapa</a> around 1526–1527 cut Sunda off from the sea and from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a> trade, which passed to the new Banten Sultanate under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan-hasanuddin">Hasanuddin</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/surawisesa">Surawisesa</a>, who reigned until about 1535, is credited in Sundanese tradition with fighting many battles to defend the remaining realm, but his successors ruled a landlocked and shrinking kingdom. In 1579 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maulana-yusuf">Maulana Yusuf of Banten</a> captured and destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakuan-pajajaran">Pakuan Pajajaran</a>. According to tradition the stone throne of the Sundanese kings was carried off to Banten, where a coronation stone still lies at the old palace site of Surosowan, and the last king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raga-mulya">Raga Mulya</a>, died in the western hills soon afterwards. Some Sundanese traditions hold that the royal regalia passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumedang">Sumedang</a> Larang, which became the main Sundanese successor in the east until it came under Mataram. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-java">West Java</a> was thereafter divided between Banten, Cirebon and Mataram and later fell under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> rule.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Sunda Kingdom was the last major <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-buddhist">Hindu-Buddhist</a> state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-java">West Java</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a>-exporting realm that stayed outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a> control and made the 1522 treaty with Portugal, and whose fall to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banten">Banten</a> in 1579 ended pre-Islamic kingship in the region while leaving a lasting Sundanese language, literature and identity.</em></p>

